projectDetails.summary:
Have you ever imagined what would happen if a huge asteroid came toward Earth? 
It sounds like a science fiction movie, but it is actually something that scientists around the world take very seriously.
The challenge we chose for the NASA Space Apps Hackathon is called Meteor Madness. 
It is based on the idea that an asteroid could pass very close to Earth in the near future, and we must be prepared.
Potential consequences of a large asteroid impact include:
Powerful earthquakes that destroy buildings and reshape the ground;
Massive tsunamis if it fell into the ocean;
Giant craters like the ones we see on the Moon;
Climate changes caused by the dust raised during the impact;
NASA already collects thousands of data points about asteroids, including their orbits, sizes, and speeds, while the USGS records and studies earthquakes worldwide. However, these pieces of information remain separated, stored in technical databases that are difficult for most people to understand.
Our project aims to combine all this information into an easy-to-use, interactive, and educational tool — something anyone, from a child to an adult, can understand.
The Impact will be an interactive educational game developed in Scratch, based on real data from NASA and the USGS. The goal is to allow children, students, educators, and researchers to simulate the behavior of an asteroid on a collision course with Earth and to learn mitigation strategies inspired by NASA’s planetary defense studies.

